================
Native VLAN Change

Why we did this
================
In IEEE 802.1Q trunking, one VLAN is designated as the Native VLAN.

Frames belonging to the Native VLAN are sent untagged across the trunk link.

By default, Cisco switches use VLAN 1 as the Native VLAN.

Attackers can exploit this behavior using VLAN Hopping attacks, especially Double Tagging attacks, to gain access to traffic from other VLANs.

Since VLAN 1 is also commonly used for management and default switch operations, it becomes an attractive target.

To reduce this risk, administrators change the Native VLAN from VLAN 1 to an unused VLAN.

What will happen
================
1. A dedicated unused VLAN is created.
2. The Native VLAN on all trunk links is changed to this unused VLAN.
3. User devices are not placed in the Native VLAN.
4. Untagged traffic is placed into the unused Native VLAN.
5. VLAN Hopping risks are reduced.

Example:

Before Change

Native VLAN = VLAN 1

Trunk Link:
Switch A -------- Switch B

Risk:
An attacker may exploit VLAN 1 using Double Tagging attacks.


After Change

Native VLAN = VLAN 999

Trunk Link:
Switch A -------- Switch B

Result:
Untagged traffic enters VLAN 999 which contains no users and no resources.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Reduces VLAN Hopping attack risk.
2. Removes dependence on VLAN 1.
3. Improves trunk security.
4. Follows Cisco security best practices.
5. Easy to implement.
6. Works with existing VLAN designs.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Must be configured on both ends of a trunk.
2. Native VLAN mismatches can cause connectivity issues.
3. Does not completely eliminate VLAN attacks.
4. Requires proper documentation.
5. Additional VLAN security measures are still needed.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Changing the Native VLAN reduces risk, but attackers may still attempt VLAN Hopping through trunk negotiation mechanisms.

Administrators realized that preventing VLAN Hopping entirely requires disabling unnecessary trunk negotiations and explicitly controlling VLAN access.

This led to:

VLAN Hopping Prevention

which combines:
- Native VLAN Change
- DTP Disable
- Access Port Hardening
- Trunk Port Hardening

to prevent unauthorized VLAN access.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Create the Native VLAN

Switch(config)# vlan 999
Switch(config-vlan)# name NATIVE_VLAN
Switch(config-vlan)# exit


Configure the Trunk

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode trunk
Switch(config-if)# switchport trunk native vlan 999
Switch(config-if)# switchport nonegotiate
Switch(config-if)# end

(Note: Configure the same Native VLAN on the switch at the other end of the trunk.)

Verification
============

show interfaces trunk

show vlan brief

Expected Result
===============

Native VLAN:
------------
999

Trunk Status:
-------------
Operational

VLAN 1:
-------
No longer used as Native VLAN

Security:
---------
Reduced risk of Double Tagging and VLAN Hopping attacks

Summary
=======

Problem:
VLAN 1 as the default Native VLAN can be exploited during VLAN Hopping attacks.

Solution:
Move the Native VLAN to an unused VLAN such as VLAN 999.

Benefit:
Improves trunk security and reduces attack opportunities.

Limitation:
Native VLAN attacks are reduced but not completely eliminated.

